          "outputs": "The Advisory Committee gave in-depth consideration to a series of detailed reports regarding expected hydraulic, economic and social outcomes associated with each of the proposed and additional options.  The Advisory Committee reviewed these outcomes in its role as advocate for the community.  The recommendations of the committee reflect community opinion in relation to all outcomes.\r\n1. Council proceed with raising Hinze Dam for flood mitigation as the first preference for flood mitigation works.\r\n2. In the event that Council take a decision in the future to further raise Hinze Dam for water supply, the same amount of flood storage space achieved by the current Hinze Dam flood mitigation option be maintained in the further raising for the dam.\r\n3. If the raising of Hinze Dam for flood mitigation is expected to take longer than five years, Council gives consideration to implementing recommendations 4 and 5 below as interim flood mitigation measures.\r\n4. The Committee also supports modifying the Benowa Flood Channel to increase the flow of floodwater taking this shorter alternative route during a major flood event.\r\n5. The committee also supports dredging of the Lower Nerang River to increase the flow of floodwater escaping the system in a major flood event.\r\n6. Modifications to Vai Roma and Chevron Island Bridge not be pursued as a flood mitigation option.  However, the flood sensitive designs recommended in the hydraulic reports be considered in any future capital works programs involving these bridges.\r\n7. Council's flood mitigation decision be implemented as soon as possible.\r\n8. The implementation of a flood mitigation project will not result in a relaxation of the application of the Flood Affected Areas Constraint Code in the development assessment process.  Any benefits achieved through flood mitigation works will not be eroded through relaxations in requirements on future developments.\r\n10. Council adopts a City-wide approach towards funding flood mitigation options as being the most practical to implement, equitable and affordable approach, consistent with the majority of funding decisions taken by Council.",
